---
import '../styles/global.css';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navigation from '../components/Navigation.astro';
import BackButton from '../components/BackButton.astro';
import Journal from '../components/Journal.tsx';
---

<BaseLayout title="Brain Dump" description="Write down your thoughts and let them go. Everything stays private on your device.">
  <div class="page">
    <div class="page__header">
      <BackButton />
      <h1 class="page__title">Brain Dump</h1>
      <p class="page__desc">Write it down, lock it away, let it go</p>
    </div>
    <Journal client:load />
  </div>

  <article class="article-guide">
    <div class="article-guide__inner">
      <h2>The Science of Bedtime Brain Dumping and Cognitive Offloading</h2>
      <p>
        When you climb into bed at night, your sensory environment quiets down, leaving your conscious mind with zero external distractions. For millions of people, this sudden quiet opens the floodgates to unfinished tasks, unresolved conversations, and tomorrow's responsibilities. Instead of easing into slow-wave delta sleep, the brain remains trapped in high-frequency beta wave rumination.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>Brain Dump</strong> method counters this through a psychological mechanism known as <em>cognitive offloading</em>. By physically externalizing mental clutter onto a page, you signal to your working memory that these items have been recorded and safely preserved. This releases the nervous system from the burden of constant rehearsal, allowing the brain to power down.
      </p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <strong>The Baylor University Sleep Study:</strong> In a landmark study published by Dr. Michael Scullin in the <em>Journal of Experimental Psychology</em>, participants who spent five minutes writing down a specific to-do list before bed fell asleep an average of 9 minutes faster than those who wrote about completed tasks. The researchers found that offloading future obligations physically reduces cognitive arousal and accelerates sleep onset latency.
      </div>

      <h3>Why Bedtime Writing Works</h3>
      <div class="guide-card-grid">
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>The Zeigarnik Effect</h4>
          <p>The human brain tends to remember unfinished or interrupted tasks far more vividly than completed ones. Writing these open loops down provides cognitive closure so your brain stops spinning on them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>Emotional De-escalation</h4>
          <p>Putting distressing emotions into clear written language transfers neural processing from the reactive amygdala to the analytical prefrontal cortex, reducing somatic tension and heart rate.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>Zero-Tracking Privacy</h4>
          <p>True psychological release requires total vulnerability. Fall Asleep stores every journal entry strictly inside your browser's local storage with zero cloud transmission, ensuring total privacy.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>How to Practice a 5-Minute Bedtime Brain Dump</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Set a brief boundary:</strong> Give yourself just 3 to 5 minutes to write. This is not about writing an elaborate autobiography; it is an unedited brain purge to clear out working memory.</li>
        <li><strong>Dump raw unfinished tasks:</strong> Write down any upcoming deadlines, messages you need to send tomorrow, or errands lingering in your mind. Be as specific as possible (for example: "Call pharmacy at 10 AM", "Draft morning email").</li>
        <li><strong>Acknowledge intrusive worries:</strong> If a persistent fear or relationship stress is looping in your mind, summarize it in a single objective sentence: "I am feeling uncertain about tomorrow's presentation." Acknowledging it on the page takes away its power to interrupt your sleep.</li>
        <li><strong>Lock it away and step away:</strong> Once written, consciously tell yourself: "Everything I need to remember is safely saved. There is nothing more I can accomplish tonight. My only job now is to rest."</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="guide-disclaimer">
        <strong>Wellness Notice:</strong> Bedtime journaling is a non-clinical self-care and mental hygiene habit intended to support calm sleep. It is not psychotherapy, clinical counseling, or a medical treatment for clinical insomnia, generalized anxiety disorder, or severe depression. If racing thoughts or distress consistently hinder your quality of life, please consult a qualified mental health professional.
      </div>

      <h3>Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How does writing before bed help me fall asleep faster?</summary>
          <p>Writing acts as an external storage drive for your brain. By recording tomorrow's obligations and nagging thoughts, you alleviate the burden on your working memory, which lowers nocturnal cortisol levels and allows parasympathetic relaxation to take over.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Is it better to write a to-do list or write about my feelings?</summary>
          <p>Clinical sleep research shows that writing a specific, actionable to-do list for tomorrow is actually more effective for speeding up sleep onset than general expressive journaling. When you write actionable tasks, you remove the anticipatory anxiety of forgetting something important in the morning.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can using a screen for journaling keep me awake?</summary>
          <p>Bright screens can suppress melatonin, which is why Fall Asleep is built with an ultra-low luminance, true-dark palette and minimal contrast. However, we recommend dimming your device brightness to its lowest setting and enabling your device's night-shift warm color temperature.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Where are my journal entries stored?</summary>
          <p>All notes written in the Brain Dump tool are saved strictly in your device's browser local storage (localStorage). No data is ever sent to a remote server, third-party database, or cloud infrastructure. Clearing your browser cache or deleting the entry locally removes it permanently.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What should I do if writing down my thoughts makes me more anxious?</summary>
          <p>If expressive journaling causes you to fixate on distress, stop writing and switch to a structured to-do list instead. Alternatively, switch to an active cognitive distraction like our Cognitive Shuffle or 4-7-8 Breathing exercises to gently redirect your mind away from introspective loops.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <Navigation slot="nav" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    max-width: 40rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1rem 0.5rem 6rem;
  }

  .page__header {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .page__title {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0.25rem;
    color: var(--color-drift-text);
  }

  .page__desc {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-drift-text-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
